   \code{category$dictionary} \tab \code{NULL} \cr
   \code{category$overwrite} \tab \code{TRUE} \cr
   \code{category$policy} \tab \code{appusage_category_policy()} \cr
   \code{category$profile} \tab \code{NULL} \cr
   \code{category$manifest} \tab \code{NULL} \cr
}

\itemize{
\item \code{category$enabled}: Logical: attach category labels.
\item \code{category$dictionary}: Bundled dictionary; alternatively a dictionary data frame or Excel path.
\item \code{category$overwrite}: Logical: replace existing nonmissing category values.
\item \code{category$policy}: Validated classification/review policy from its constructor.
\item \code{category$profile}: Optional frozen result of \code{profile_uncoded_apps()}.
\item \code{category$manifest}: Optional explicit source/person/observation/primary-source manifest for profiling.
}

Construct \code{category$policy} with the following five arguments. The default
policy version is \code{"category-policy-1"}; it is separate from both the
manual dictionary version and the package version.\tabular{ll}{
   Setting \tab Default \cr
   \code{share_threshold} \tab \code{0.05} \cr
   \code{prevalence_threshold} \tab \code{0.01} \cr
   \code{auto_other} \tab \code{TRUE} \cr
   \code{other_l1} \tab \code{"Others"} \cr
   \code{other_l2} \tab \code{"Unclassified"} \cr
}

\itemize{
\item \code{share_threshold}: Proportion from 0 to 1 inclusive; a person's maximum primary-file foreground share at or above this value triggers review.
\item \code{prevalence_threshold}: Proportion from 0 to 1 inclusive; positive-foreground person prevalence at or above this value triggers review.
\item \code{auto_other}: Logical: assign the residual pair to measurable unknown apps below both thresholds.
\item \code{other_l1}: Nonempty character residual L1 label.
\item \code{other_l2}: Nonempty character residual L2 label.
}

Thresholds use inclusive OR logic. The fixed policy uses a person's maximum
share across primary observations and positive-foreground presence across
people. Missing metrics remain pending. A manifest identifies primary files,
shared observations and people explicitly; file counts are not person counts.
Dictionary matches and residual assignments are reported separately.
