\section{Execution and recovery}{

Execution settings control resources, continuation and cache verification.
They are distinct from scientific criteria.\tabular{ll}{
   Setting \tab Default \cr
   \code{execution$parallel} \tab \code{FALSE} \cr
   \code{execution$workers} \tab \code{1L} \cr
   \code{execution$strict} \tab \code{FALSE} \cr
   \code{execution$progress} \tab \code{TRUE} \cr
   \code{execution$resume} \tab \code{TRUE} \cr
   \code{execution$overwrite} \tab \code{FALSE} \cr
   \code{execution$checkpoint_every} \tab \code{100L} \cr
   \code{execution$source_verification} \tab \code{"content"} \cr
}

\itemize{
\item \code{execution$parallel}: Logical: request file-level parallel work.
\item \code{execution$workers}: Positive integer file-worker request; runtime resource limits may reduce actual workers.
\item \code{execution$strict}: Logical: stop the batch/workflow on a failed source when TRUE; otherwise retain its diagnostic row and continue.
\item \code{execution$progress}: Logical: print progress messages.
\item \code{execution$resume}: Logical: reuse compatible, verified existing work.
\item \code{execution$overwrite}: Logical: allow requested replacement; ordinary unchanged resume uses FALSE.
\item \code{execution$checkpoint_every}: Positive integer source-record interval for checkpoints.
\item \code{execution$source_verification}: \code{content} checks source content; \code{metadata} uses source size/mtime; \code{cache_only} uses saved caches without raw-source comparison.
}

Planning with \code{verify = "metadata"} is a metadata preview. Use
\code{\link[=validate_appusage_project]{validate_appusage_project()}} with \code{verify = "content"} for content delivery
checks. An invalid upstream cache in cache-only mode is blocked rather than
reparsed. In lower-level batch APIs the worker argument is \code{n_cores};
\code{\link[=run_appusage_projects]{run_appusage_projects()}} uses \code{workers} (default eight) while executing
projects serially. The examples use one worker; the installed
\code{demo("synthetic-project", package = "appusageR")} demonstrates parallel work.
}

\section{Configuration and stage ownership}{

Pass \code{config} to the workflow instead of simultaneously supplying legacy
scientific/execution arguments. Unknown fields are rejected. A saved
configuration contains requested settings; the plan and \code{run_record} contain
proposed and actual actions. Parsing changes affect downstream work;
reconstruction/daily changes affect research-data and dependent stages;
coverage-QC and category changes have their own stage contracts. Duration
thresholds also synchronize persisted anomaly labels. Source selection,
relationship selection and category coverage are separate concerns.

Functions also document their own controls, such as \code{verify} in validation,
\code{strategy}/\code{window} options in \code{\link[=audit_appusage_days]{audit_appusage_days()}}, and
\code{source_qc_config} in \code{\link[=qc_appusage_anomalies]{qc_appusage_anomalies()}}. Those are not additional
fields accepted by \code{\link[=appusage_config]{appusage_config()}}.
}

